  If you use any part of this repository, please cite the manuscript rather
  than only the repository. Once the preprint appears on arXiv, replace the
  text below with its arXiv identifier and add its DOI.


type: software
title: >-
  Persistent Negative Lag-One Dependence in Locally Detrended Prime-Gap
  Windows up to N = 10^9, with Absence of Significant Positive Autocorrelation
  up to K = 1024
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-10-02
authors:
  - family-names: BERRAMDANE
    given-names: Reddouane
    alias: reddoma742
license: CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0
license-url: https://github.com/reddoma742/prime-gaps-autocorrelation/blob/main/LICENSE
repository-code: https://github.com/reddoma742/prime-gaps-autocorrelation
url: https://github.com/reddoma742/prime-gaps-autocorrelation
keywords:
  - prime gaps
  - autocorrelation
  - Hardy-Littlewood pair density
  - Lemke-Oliver-Soundararajan conjecture
  - Chebyshev function
  - number theory
  - 11N05
  - 62M10
  - 11N56
abstract: >-
  Analysis of the gap sequence g_n = p_{n+1} - p_n of consecutive primes with an
  exact segmented sieve up to N = 10^9, which yields 50847532 even gaps. Within
  locally detrended logarithmic windows the lag-one autocorrelation rho_1 is
  negative in all ten windows spanning [10^4, 10^9), and |rho_1| decays like
  (log p)^-c with a weighted fit c = 1.28 +/- 0.05. The quadratic exponent c = 2
  is excluded between 3.7 and 14.4 standard errors, and at 6.6 standard errors on
  the bootstrap scale. Out to lag K = 1024 no individual lag shows significant
  positive autocorrelation: 255 of 1024 lags are positive, the largest being
  +3.86e-4 against a Bonferroni threshold of 5.70e-4. Without detrending the
  same correlogram shows 1016 positive lags with a maximum of +4.24e-3, which
  identifies the naive signal as a non-stationarity artifact. An
  inclusion-exclusion extension of the Hardy-Littlewood pair-density heuristic
  reproduces the observed pair ratios to 0.37 percent mean absolute error with
  no fitted constants. This repository holds the C++ and Python pipelines, the
  16 CSV data files with their SHA-256 checksums, the run logs, and the LaTeX
  sources of the manuscript.